    "html": "<h1>EU AI Act compliance checklist for deployers (2026–2027)</h1>\n<p>A <strong>deployer</strong> under the EU AI Act is any natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body that uses an AI system under its authority, except where the system is used in the course of a personal, non-professional activity (Art. 3(6); Recital 13). The decisive point: you are a deployer whether or not you built the system yourself — running a third-party model or off-the-shelf tool inside your own business makes you a deployer (Art. 26). For 2026–2027, the practical priority is to stand up the <em>foundational</em>, always-on duties now — AI literacy, an AI system inventory with risk classification, using systems per the provider's instructions, transparency to affected people, and monitoring — rather than waiting for the Annex III high-risk obligations, which the Digital Omnibus moved from 2 August 2026 to <strong>2 December 2027</strong> (see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-timeline-2024-2027\">full application timeline</a>). </p>\n<h2>1. Who qualifies as a deployer (Art. 26)</h2>\n<p>The AI Act defines a <strong>deployer</strong> in Art. 3(6) and attaches the operating obligations in Art. 26. The defining test is <em>use under your authority</em>: if your organisation puts an AI system into operation for a professional purpose — recruitment scoring, document triage, a customer chatbot, code review, or an AI agent acting on your behalf — you are the deployer, regardless of who developed it (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 26</a>).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>You don't have to be the developer.</strong> A company that only consumes a GPT-class model via API, or runs an off-the-shelf tool, is still a deployer (Art. 26(1)). The provider's job is to place a conforming system on the market; your job is to use it correctly.</li><li><strong>Personal, non-professional use is out of scope.</strong> Art. 3(6) excludes individuals using AI outside work.</li><li><strong>One entity can wear two hats.</strong> If you both build and deploy AI, you owe provider duties for what you place on the market (Art. 25) and deployer duties for what you use (Art. 26). For the full split of who owes what, see <a href=\"/blog/provider-vs-deployer-responsibilities\">provider vs deployer responsibilities</a>. </li><li><strong>Non-EU providers need an authorised representative.</strong> Where the provider is established outside the EU, Art. 27 requires an EU-based authorised representative; the deployer's duties are unchanged.</li></ul>\n<h3>Provider vs deployer at a glance</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Provider (Art. 25)</th><th>Deployer (Art. 26)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Core role</td><td>Puts the AI system on the EU market / into service</td><td>Uses the system under its own authority</td></tr><tr><td>Owns the build</td><td>Yes — risk management (Art. 9), data governance (Art. 10), technical documentation (Art. 11)</td><td>No — consumes the provider's output</td></tr><tr><td>Must give instructions</td><td>Yes — Art. 13 transparency to deployers</td><td>Receives and follows them — Art. 26(1)</td></tr><tr><td>High-risk pre-use step</td><td>Conformity assessment (Art. 43)</td><td>Fundamental-rights & intended-use check (Art. 26(2))</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>2. Your deployer compliance checklist (2026–2027)</h2>\n<p>The list below is grouped by <em>when</em> each duty bites. Items tagged <strong>Now</strong> are not postponed by the Digital Omnibus; items tagged <strong>2 Dec 2027</strong> are the high-risk (Annex III) deployer duties whose application was delayed. As of July 2026, the Council of the EU gave final approval to the Digital Omnibus on 29 June 2026; the new dates take effect once the amending regulation is published in the Official Journal, which the Commission targeted before 2 August 2026 (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-act-and-digital-omnibus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — AI Act & Digital Omnibus</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Now — build the baseline (not date-contingent)</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Inventory every AI system and classify its risk (Art. 6).</strong> The classification analysis is <em>not</em> postponed. A system is high-risk under Art. 6(1) (Annex I regulated products) or Art. 6(2) (Annex III sensitive domains such as employment, education, essential services, biometrics, and law enforcement). Draft Commission guidelines on classification were published on 19 May 2026.</li><li><strong>Evidence AI literacy for staff who operate or use AI (Art. 4).</strong> Applies since 2 February 2025 — keep training records. This is the practical front door to compliance and is untouched by the Omnibus.</li><li><strong>Use each system in line with the provider's instructions for use (Art. 26(1)).</strong> The general deployer duty: operate within the documented intended purpose and constraints.</li><li><strong>Be transparent to the people affected (Art. 26(6) + Art. 50).</strong> From 2 August 2026, tell natural persons when they interact with an AI system and when content is artificially generated or manipulated. Visible labelling of chatbots and deepfakes is <em>not</em> delayed (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — regulatory framework</a>).</li><li><strong>Secure and retain the provider's technical documentation and instructions (Art. 11 / Art. 13).</strong> Deployers are entitled to receive the information needed to use the system properly; keep it as your evidence base.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>2 Dec 2027 — high-risk (Annex III) deployer duties</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Run a fundamental-rights and intended-purpose assessment before first use (Art. 26(2)).</strong> Document why the system is appropriate for your use case.</li><li><strong>Assign a qualified person and operate human oversight (Art. 26(3) + Art. 14).</strong> Humans must stay in the loop for high-risk systems.</li><li><strong>Ensure input data is relevant and representative (Art. 26(4)).</strong> Poor input undermines the provider's accuracy and robustness claims (Art. 15).</li><li><strong>Monitor performance, keep logs, and report serious incidents (Art. 26(5) + Art. 12 logging + Art. 72).</strong> Serious incidents must be reported to the provider and, where required, to the market surveillance authority.</li><li><strong>Register in the EU high-risk database (Art. 26(7) + Art. 49 / Art. 71).</strong> High-risk deployers must register their use in the public EU database.</li><li><strong>Confirm conformity is in place (Art. 43 / Art. 47).</strong> You rely on the provider's EU declaration of conformity; verify it covers your intended use.</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p><strong>What changed vs what didn't.</strong> The Digital Omnibus delays the <em>application</em> of the high-risk regime — including the provider's foundational duties (Art. 9 risk management, Art. 10 data governance, Art. 11 technical documentation) and the deployer's high-risk duties above — to 2 December 2027 for Annex III systems. It does <strong>not</strong> rewrite those articles, and it leaves Art. 4 (literacy), Art. 5 (prohibitions), Art. 50 (transparency), and the GPAI rules exactly where they were. Annex I (AI embedded in regulated products) moves to 2 August 2028. This depends on the amending regulation's publication; until then the original 2 August 2026 date remains the law on the books.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>3. Documentation & record-keeping that survives an audit</h2>\n<p>A checklist you can't evidence is only a wish. Build a single, audit-ready evidence store so that, if a market surveillance authority asks, you can show <em>what</em> you deployed, <em>why</em> it is (or isn't) high-risk, and <em>how</em> you operate it.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Classification file (Art. 6):</strong> the system, its intended purpose, the Annex it maps to (if any), and the reasoning. Note the 19 May 2026 draft guidelines.</li><li><strong>Instructions for use + technical documentation (Art. 13 / Art. 11):</strong> the provider's documents you received and relied on.</li><li><strong>Fundamental-rights assessment (Art. 26(2)):</strong> your pre-use write-up for high-risk use.</li><li><strong>Human-oversight design (Art. 14):</strong> who watches the system and the override path.</li><li><strong>Logs & monitoring records (Art. 12 / Art. 26(5)):</strong> runtime logs and review notes.</li><li><strong>Incident records (Art. 72):</strong> any serious incident, the report, and the provider notification.</li><li><strong>Registration confirmation (Art. 49 / Art. 71):</strong> the EU database entry for high-risk use.</li><li><strong>AI-literacy evidence (Art. 4):</strong> training attendance and scope.</li></ul>\n<p>Treat this like any other control evidence. The shape is the same lineage your GRC, SOX, or ISO 42001 programme already maintains — one register, versioned, with owners and dates — so the AI Act evidence is a <em>subset</em> of your existing audit trail rather than a parallel spreadsheet. A governance-register pattern for this is covered in later guidance on AI Act × ISO 42001.</p>\n",
