    "description": "The EU AI Act applies in phases. This timeline shows which dates survive the Digital Omnibus (entry into force 1 Aug 2024, Art. 5 / Art. 4 on 2 Feb 2025, GPAI on 2 Aug 2025, Art. 50 transparency on 2 Aug 2026) and which moved (Annex III high-risk to 2 Dec 2027, Annex I to 2 Aug 2028).",
    "html": "<h1>EU AI Act timeline 2024–2027: dates that survive the Omnibus</h1>\n<p>The EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) does not switch on all at once — it applies in phases set out in Art. 113. Its backbone timeline is intact: the Regulation entered into force on 1 August 2024, prohibited practices and the AI-literacy duty applied from 2 February 2025, and the general-purpose AI (GPAI) rules applied from 2 August 2025. What the Digital Omnibus moved is narrow: the high-risk obligations that were due to apply on 2 August 2026 are postponed — to <strong>2 December 2027</strong> for stand-alone Annex III systems and to <strong>2 August 2028</strong> for AI embedded in regulated products (Annex I). The practical takeaway for any EU-facing team is that the <em>classification</em> and <em>baseline</em> duties are not deferred — only the date the high-risk regime's substantive obligations bite. A deployer still needs an AI inventory, a risk classification, AI-literacy evidence, and transparency to affected people now (see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">deployer compliance checklist</a>). </p>\n<h2>1. The phased application timeline</h2>\n<p>The Act's application schedule is fixed in Art. 113 of Regulation (EU) 2024/1689. The table below lists every milestone, its legal basis, and whether the Digital Omnibus left it, moved it, or added to it.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What becomes applicable</th><th>Legal basis</th><th>Status after the Digital Omnibus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Aug 2024</td><td>Regulation enters into force</td><td>Art. 113(1)</td><td>Unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>2 Feb 2025</td><td>Prohibited practices; AI-literacy duty; governance & penalty framework</td><td>Art. 5, Art. 4, Ch. VII–IX</td><td>Unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>2 Aug 2025</td><td>GPAI obligations for providers (transparency, technical documentation, copyright, systemic-risk mitigation)</td><td>Art. 53, Art. 55</td><td>Unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>2 Aug 2026</td><td>Transparency obligations — disclose AI interaction; label deepfakes / AI-generated content</td><td>Art. 50</td><td>Unchanged (still applies)</td></tr><tr><td>2 Dec 2026</td><td><strong>New</strong> prohibitions added to Art. 5 (non-consensual intimate imagery, CSAM); Art. 50(2) watermarking grace for systems placed before 2 Aug 2026</td><td>Art. 5 (amended), Art. 50(2)</td><td>Added by the Omnibus</td></tr><tr><td>2 Dec 2027</td><td>High-risk obligations for stand-alone Annex III systems (risk management, data governance, technical documentation, human oversight, registration, monitoring)</td><td>Art. 6(2) + Annex III, read with Art. 9–15, 26, 43, 49, 71</td><td><strong>Postponed from 2 Aug 2026</strong></td></tr><tr><td>2 Aug 2028</td><td>High-risk obligations for AI embedded as safety components in Annex I regulated products</td><td>Art. 6(1) + Annex I</td><td><strong>Postponed from 2 Aug 2027</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The classification logic itself — <em>whether</em> a system is high-risk under Art. 6 — is not postponed; the delay only changes <em>when</em> the high-risk duties must be in place. For how a system gets classified, see <a href=\"/blog/high-risk-annex-iii-classification\">Annex III classification logic</a>. </p>\n<h2>2. The dates that did not move</h2>\n<p>Four milestones survive the Omnibus exactly as originally enacted. Treat these as the always-on backbone:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>1 August 2024 — entry into force (Art. 113).</strong> No immediate obligations for most organisations, but every later phase is measured from this date.</li><li><strong>2 February 2025 — prohibited practices (Art. 5) and AI literacy (Art. 4).</strong> The eight banned practices (manipulation, social scoring, untargeted facial-image scraping, emotion recognition in workplaces/schools, certain biometric categorisation, real-time remote biometric ID, and others) have applied since February 2025, as has the duty to promote AI literacy among staff who use or operate AI systems (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — regulatory framework</a>).</li><li><strong>2 August 2025 — GPAI rules (Art. 53 / Art. 55).</strong> Providers of general-purpose AI models owe transparency, technical documentation, and copyright-policy obligations; capable models carry enhanced systemic-risk mitigation. Unchanged.</li><li><strong>2 August 2026 — transparency obligations (Art. 50).</strong> The duty to tell natural persons when they interact with an AI system, and to label deepfakes and other AI-generated content, applies from 2 August 2026 <strong>as originally scheduled</strong>. The Omnibus did not move this date — it is still in force, and it is the main new obligation that actually lands in August 2026 (see the FAQ on what hits that date).</li></ul>\n<p>These four are the \"foundational\" pillars: entry into force, prohibitions, literacy, GPAI, and Art. 50 transparency. None of them was deferred.</p>\n<h2>3. The dates that moved, and what they mean</h2>\n<p>The Digital Omnibus changes only the <em>application</em> of the high-risk regime, in two steps:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Annex III — stand-alone high-risk → 2 December 2027</strong> (postponed from 2 August 2026). This covers the eight high-risk domains listed in Annex III: biometric identification and categorisation, critical infrastructure, education and vocational training, employment and worker management, essential private and public services and benefits, law enforcement, migration, asylum and border control, and the administration of justice and democratic processes (Art. 6(2) read with Annex III).</li><li><strong>Annex I — product-integrated high-risk → 2 August 2028</strong> (postponed from 2 August 2027). This covers AI that is a safety component in products already governed by EU sectoral safety legislation — machinery, medical devices, vehicles, aviation, and marine equipment (Art. 6(1) read with Annex I).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What it means in practice.</strong> The <em>substance</em> of the high-risk regime is unchanged — risk-management system (Art. 9), data governance (Art. 10), technical documentation (Art. 11), automatic logging (Art. 12), transparency to deployers (Art. 13), human oversight (Art. 14), the deployer's fundamental-rights and intended-use assessment (Art. 26(2)), EU database registration (Art. 49 / Art. 71), and conformity assessment (Art. 43) all remain. Only the <em>date</em> they must be operational moved. The classification analysis (Art. 6) is not postponed, so mapping your systems to Annex III now is still the right move. For the full set of deployer duties and the evidence an audit expects, see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">deployer compliance checklist</a>.  For everything the Omnibus changed and what it left alone, see <a href=\"/blog/what-digital-omnibus-changed\">What the Digital Omnibus actually changed</a>. </p>\n<p><strong>Status and uncertainty (as of July 2026).</strong> The co-legislators reached political agreement on the Digital Omnibus on 7 May 2026, and the Council approved it on 29 June 2026 (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/eu-agrees-simplify-ai-rules-boost-innovation-and-ban-nudification-apps-protect-citizens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — Digital Omnibus</a>; <a href=\"https://www.europarl.europa.eu/legislative-train/package-digital-package/file-digital-omnibus-on-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">European Parliament — Digital Omnibus</a>). The new dates take legal effect only once the amending regulation is published in the Official Journal, which the Commission targeted before 2 August 2026. Until that publication, the original 2 August 2026 date for Annex III remains the law on the books, so teams should plan to the new dates but track the Official Journal for the precise entry into force of the amending regulation.</p>\n",
