    "description": "Compare the EU AI Act penalty tiers (Art. 99: up to €35M or 7%) with the GDPR (Art. 83: up to €20M or 4%). Explains who each regime fines — deployers under the AI Act vs controllers/processors under the GDPR — and why the two can apply in parallel to the same AI system.",
    "html": "<h1>EU AI Act vs GDPR penalty tiers: what deployers should know</h1>\n<p>The <strong>EU AI Act</strong> (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) and the <strong>GDPR</strong> (Regulation (EU) 2016/679) each impose their own administrative fines through separate legal bases — the AI Act via <strong>Art. 99</strong>, the GDPR via <strong>Art. 83</strong> (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EU AI Act, Art. 99</a>; <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-83/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GDPR, Art. 83</a>). The headline ceilings differ: the AI Act tops out at €35,000,000 or 7% of global annual turnover (for prohibited practices), the GDPR at €20,000,000 or 4%. The conclusion that matters for deployers: the two regimes <strong>can apply at the same time</strong>, and because the AI Act fines by <em>operator role</em> (provider vs deployer) while the GDPR fines by <em>controller vs processor</em>, a single AI system that processes personal data can make you a deployer under one law and a controller under the other — exposed to both ladders. The practical, <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">deployer-focused compliance checklist</a> maps each underlying duty to its article. </p>\n<h2>1. The two penalty ladders side by side</h2>\n<p>Both regimes calculate the fine as <em>whichever is higher</em> of a fixed euro amount or a percentage of total worldwide annual turnover for the preceding financial year. They differ in how many tiers they have and, crucially, in <strong>who each tier reaches</strong>.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>EU AI Act — Art. 99</th><th>GDPR — Art. 83</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Top tier</strong></td><td>Up to <strong>€35,000,000 or 7%</strong> — infringements of the prohibited practices in <strong>Art. 5</strong> (and certain other specified infringements) (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Art. 99</a>)</td><td>Up to <strong>€20,000,000 or 4%</strong> — basic principles for processing (Arts 5–7, 9), data-subject rights (Arts 12–22), third-country transfers (Arts 44–49), and non-compliance with an authority's order (<a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-83/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Art. 83(5)–(6)</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Middle tier</strong></td><td>Up to <strong>€15,000,000 or 3%</strong> — obligations of providers, authorised representatives, importers, distributors, and <strong>deployers (Art. 26)</strong>, plus transparency duties (Art. 50) (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Art. 99(4)</a>)</td><td>Up to <strong>€10,000,000 or 2%</strong> — controller/processor obligations (Arts 8, 11, 25–39, 42, 43) and certification/monitoring-body duties (<a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-83/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Art. 83(4)</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lower tier</strong></td><td>Up to <strong>€7,500,000 or 1%</strong> — supplying incorrect, incomplete, or misleading information to notified bodies or national authorities (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Art. 99(5)</a>)</td><td>(folded into the two tiers above; no separate third tier)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Who it reaches</strong></td><td>Operators by <em>role</em>: providers, deployers, authorised representatives, importers, distributors, notified bodies</td><td><em>Controllers</em> and <em>processors</em> — any natural/legal person (incl. employers) that determines or acts on the purposes and means of processing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SME treatment</strong></td><td>Fines capped at <strong>the lower of</strong> the amount or the percentage for SMEs and start-ups (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Art. 99(6)</a>)</td><td><strong>No general SME discount</strong> — the €20M/4% ceiling applies regardless of size</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Enforcer</strong></td><td>Market surveillance authorities (Member States), coordinated through the AI Office</td><td>Data protection supervisory authorities (Member States), coordinated through the EDPB</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<ul><li>The AI Act's highest tier is <strong>narrow</strong>: it is reserved for the banned practices in <strong>Art. 5</strong> (e.g. social scoring, untargeted facial-image scraping, certain workplace emotion recognition), which have been enforceable since February 2025 (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — regulatory framework</a>). Most day-to-day deployer obligations sit in the €15M/3% tier.</li><li>The GDPR's two tiers split on <em>what</em> was breached, not <em>who</em>: mishandling the core principles or individuals' rights reaches the €20M/4% ceiling; narrower controller/processor housekeeping reaches €10M/2% (<a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-83/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Art. 83(4)–(5)</a>).</li><li>As of July 2026, the Digital Omnibus has renumbered several cross-references inside Art. 99; the <em>amounts and percentages above are unchanged</em>, but verify the exact article list against the current consolidated text before relying on a specific sub-paragraph.</li></ul>\n<h2>2. Who actually gets fined</h2>\n<p>The two laws cut the same company along <strong>different axes</strong>, and that mismatch is what catches deployers out.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Under the AI Act, you are fined for the role you play.</strong> A <em>deployer</em> is anyone who uses an AI system under its own authority for a professional purpose, whether or not it built the system (<strong>Art. 3(6)</strong>; <strong>Art. 26</strong>). A deployer's own duties — using the system per the provider's instructions (<strong>Art. 26(1)</strong>), the high-risk fundamental-rights assessment (<strong>Art. 26(2)</strong>), human oversight (<strong>Art. 26(3)</strong>), input-data relevance (<strong>Art. 26(4)</strong>), monitoring and logging (<strong>Art. 26(5)</strong>), and EU database registration (<strong>Art. 26(7)</strong>) — are enforced through the €15M/3% tier (<strong>Art. 99(4)(e)</strong>). If a deployer also engages a prohibited practice, the €35M/7% tier applies (<strong>Art. 99(3)</strong>).</li><li><strong>Under the GDPR, you are fined for the data role you play.</strong> A team running AI on personal data is usually a <em>controller</em> (it decides the purposes and means) or, when acting on a customer's instructions, a <em>processor</em> (<strong>Art. 4(7)–(8)</strong>). Either way, the GDPR's Art. 83 ceilings apply to that entity directly.</li><li><strong>The axes don't map 1:1.</strong> \"Deployer\" (AI Act) and \"controller\" (GDPR) are not the same concept. One deployment can make you a deployer <em>and</em> a controller, a joint controller, or a processor, depending on the commercial and technical arrangement. The <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">provider vs deployer split</a> is only half the picture; the GDPR controller/processor split is the other. </li><li><strong>They can run in parallel.</strong> The AI Act is designed to sit <em>alongside</em> — not replace — the GDPR; Recital 10 states the AI Act is without prejudice to Regulation (EU) 2016/679. Because the two regimes rest on separate legal bases and are enforced by separate authorities (market surveillance authorities vs data protection authorities), the same AI system that processes personal data can attract both an AI Act fine and a GDPR fine for overlapping conduct. The <a href=\"/blog/gdpr-ai-act-overlapping-obligations\">overlapping obligations between the two laws</a> are covered in more depth separately. </li></ul>\n<h2>3. What it means for SMBs</h2>\n<p>The penalty ceilings are a <strong>budgeting and prioritisation reference</strong>, not a prediction of any outcome — and they are not something any tool can promise to keep you under (see the disclaimer below). Three points are worth internalising:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>The overlap zone is \"AI on personal data.\"</strong> Recruitment scoring, credit decisions, biometric matching, health or education use-cases — anywhere the model touches identifiable individuals — is exactly where the AI Act (via Art. 26) and the GDPR (via Art. 83) meet. That is where parallel exposure is realistic, not hypothetical.</li><li><strong>The clock is not uniform.</strong> Prohibited-practice penalties (€35M/7%) have applied since <strong>February 2025</strong>. AI transparency duties (<strong>Art. 50</strong>) apply from <strong>August 2026</strong>. The high-risk (Annex III) deployer duties — and the corresponding Art. 99 exposure for them — phase in from <strong>2 December 2027</strong> under the Digital Omnibus; Annex I (AI embedded in regulated products) moves to 2 August 2028 (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-act-and-digital-omnibus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — AI Act & Digital Omnibus</a>). Foundational duties such as AI literacy (<strong>Art. 4</strong>) and the provider/deployer split were not delayed. This assumes the amending regulation is published in the Official Journal; until then the original dates remain in force.</li><li><strong>The SME cap is one-sided.</strong> The AI Act caps SME fines at the <em>lower</em> of the amount or the percentage (<strong>Art. 99(6)</strong>), which can soften the blow. The GDPR has <strong>no equivalent general discount</strong> — a small company can still face the €20M/4% ceiling. Treat the AI Act cap as a partial relief, not a shield.</li><li><strong>What you can actually control is the evidence trail.</strong> Penalties are imposed only after an infringement is found; the defensible move is a single, versioned register that maps every obligation → article → owner → evidence, so a market surveillance authority or a supervisory authority gets a clear answer instead of a fire drill. A cited, audit-ready record is decision-support — it does not certify compliance and does not guarantee any fine outcome.</li></ul>\n",
