    "slug": "gpai-obligations-art-53-55",
    "title": "GPAI obligations under the EU AI Act: what Art. 53 and 55 require of model providers",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-25",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-25",
    "description": "What EU AI Act Articles 53 and 55 require of general-purpose AI (GPAI) model providers: technical documentation, training-content summary, copyright policy, and systemic-risk evaluation and monitoring. Covers the 2 August 2025 application date, the 10^25 FLOP threshold, the AI Office's role, and what deployers of third-party models owe under Art. 50.",
    "html": "<h1>GPAI obligations under the EU AI Act: what Art. 53 and 55 require of model providers</h1>\n<p>A <strong>general-purpose AI (GPAI) model</strong> is an AI model — typically a large foundation model trained at scale with self-supervision — that displays significant generality and can competently perform a wide range of distinct tasks, and that can be integrated into many downstream systems or applications (Art. 3(63)). Under the EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689), the obligations for GPAI model providers are <strong>already in force</strong>: Chapter V (Articles 51–56) applied from <strong>2 August 2025</strong>, earlier than the high-risk system regime. The core point for any team shipping or releasing a foundation model: you must now maintain technical documentation, publish a training-content summary, run a copyright-compliance policy, and — if your model is classified as presenting systemic risk — evaluate the model and monitor systemic risk. Organisations that merely consume a third-party GPAI via API are generally <strong>not</strong> GPAI providers and instead owe the transparency and correct-use duties in Arts. 26 and 50 (<a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">see the deployer checklist</a>). </p>\n<h2>1. Art. 53 — baseline obligations for every GPAI model provider</h2>\n<p>Article 53 sets the duties that apply to <strong>every</strong> provider that places a GPAI model on the EU market, regardless of model size, whether it is open or closed, and whether it presents systemic risk (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 53</a>). There are four building blocks:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Technical documentation (Art. 53(1)(a)).</strong> Draw up and maintain, for the model's whole lifecycle, the technical documentation specified in Annex XI — covering training methodology, the data used, compute, model architecture, capabilities and limitations, and <strong>evaluation results</strong>. This dossier is shared with the AI Office on request and flows downstream to system builders.</li><li><strong>Information to downstream providers (Art. 53(1)(b)).</strong> Make available to downstream providers the documentation in Annex XII — a summary of capabilities, limitations, and any safeguards — so they can meet their own EU AI Act obligations. This is the start of a compliance supply chain: a downstream high-risk application usually cannot be documented without it.</li><li><strong>Copyright-compliance policy (Art. 53(1)(c)).</strong> Put in place and make publicly available a policy to comply with Union copyright law, in particular to identify and respect rights holders' reservations of rights under Article 4(3) of the Digital Single Market Directive (the text-and-data-mining opt-out).</li><li><strong>Training-content summary (Art. 53(1)(d)).</strong> Draw up and make publicly available a sufficiently detailed summary of the content used for training, using the template the AI Office publishes. This summary is public-facing; the full Annex XI documentation is not.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Open-source is reduced, not zero.</strong> A GPAI model released under a free and open-source licence is exempt from the technical-documentation and downstream-information duties (Art. 53(1)(a)–(b)) <strong>unless</strong> it presents systemic risk — but the copyright policy (Art. 53(1)(c)) and the training-content summary (Art. 53(1)(d)) still apply (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 53(2)</a>). A systemic-risk open model loses the exemption entirely.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-EU providers need an authorised representative.</strong> A provider established outside the EU must, in writing, appoint an authorised representative established in the Union before placing the model on the market (Art. 54). The model's duties are otherwise unchanged for EU customers.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Art. 53 duty</th><th>What it means in practice</th><th>Annex / note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(1)(a) Technical documentation</td><td>Living dossier: training, data, compute, architecture, evals</td><td>Annex XI; held + shared with AI Office</td></tr><tr><td>(1)(b) Downstream info</td><td>Capability/limitation/safeguard sheet for system builders</td><td>Annex XII</td></tr><tr><td>(1)(c) Copyright policy</td><td>Respect TDM opt-outs (DSM Art. 4(3))</td><td>Publicly available</td></tr><tr><td>(1)(d) Training summary</td><td>Public, machine-readable training-content summary</td><td>AI Office template</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Status note (G1 / uncertainty).</strong> As of July 2026, the AI Office's training-content summary template and the scope guidance on what counts as a GPAI model are published and being operationalised; specific expectations continue to evolve. The European Commission's view is that GPAI rules were <strong>not</strong> altered by the 2026 Digital Omnibus, which targets the high-risk regime instead (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — regulatory framework</a>).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>2. Art. 55 & 56 — systemic-risk classification, monitoring, and codes of practice</h2>\n<p>A GPAI model presents <strong>systemic risk</strong> when it crosses a compute threshold or is designated by the AI Office. Article 51 is the gateway:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Compute presumption (Art. 51(1)(a)).</strong> A model trained with a cumulative amount of computing power of <strong>10²⁵ floating-point operations (FLOPs)</strong> or more is presumed to present systemic risk.</li><li><strong>AI Office designation (Art. 51(1)(b)).</strong> The AI Office may designate a model as systemic-risk based on criteria in Annex XIII (e.g., equivalent capabilities), regardless of FLOPs. Providers must notify once their model meets the threshold without undue delay.</li></ul>\n<p>Once a model is systemic-risk, <strong>Article 55</strong> stacks additional obligations on top of Art. 53 — this is the \"sysic monitoring\" layer:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Model evaluation, including adversarial testing (Art. 55(1)(a)).</strong> Assess the model's capabilities and evaluate it throughout its lifecycle, including red-teaming, before and after training.</li><li><strong>Systemic-risk assessment and mitigation (Art. 55(1)(b)).</strong> Assess and mitigate systemic risks, including those that emerge in the AI systems integrating the model; track and report serious incidents to the AI Office.</li><li><strong>Cybersecurity (Art. 55(1)(c)).</strong> Ensure an adequate level of protection for the model and its infrastructure against threats that could lead to systemic risk.</li><li>The AI Office may require providers to apply corrective measures where risk mitigation is inadequate (Art. 55(2)).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Codes of practice (Art. 56).</strong> The AI Office facilitates Union-level codes of practice as the primary, proportionate route to demonstrate compliance with Arts. 53 and 55; providers may instead use other adequate means, subject to Commission assessment. As of July 2026 the codes' content and adoption continue to develop, and a provider should track which commitments apply to its model tier (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-office\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — AI Office</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Who enforces this.</strong> The European AI Office — set up inside the Commission — has centralised competence to supervise and enforce GPAI rules. It can conduct evaluations of models, request information and corrective measures from providers, and apply sanctions; it also develops the methodologies used to classify systemic-risk models (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-office\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — AI Office</a>). This is different from high-risk systems, where national market-surveillance authorities lead.</p>\n<h2>3. What GPAI obligations mean for SMBs that deploy third-party models</h2>\n<p>Most SMBs do <strong>not</strong> train or release foundation models — they call a GPAI via API (e.g., an LLM behind a feature). In that case:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>You are usually a deployer, not a GPAI provider (Art. 26).</strong> Consuming a third-party model makes you the deployer of an AI <em>system</em>; the model provider (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Mistral, etc.) owes the Art. 53/55 duties. You are entitled to receive the provider's instructions and the GPAI provider's Annex XII information to operate the system correctly (Art. 26(1); Art. 53(1)(b)).</li><li><strong>Your priority is transparency (Art. 50).</strong> The interaction-level duties most teams touch — telling people when they are interacting with an AI system, and labelling deepfakes or AI-generated public-interest text — apply from <strong>2 August 2026</strong> (Art. 50(1)–(4)). A GPAI model provider must also ensure that systems integrating its model can produce the required content markings (Art. 50, para. 5). Build the disclosure into your product now.</li><li><strong>Keep the supplier's documentation as your evidence.</strong> Retain the provider's technical documentation and instructions (Art. 11 / Art. 13) and the GPAI Annex XII sheet; they are your audit trail if a market-surveillance authority asks how you use the model.</li><li><strong>You may become a provider if you go further.</strong> Fine-tuning a GPAI and releasing the weights, or releasing your own foundation model, can make you a GPAI provider owing Art. 53 (and Art. 55 if systemic-risk). And if you integrate a GPAI into a system that is high-risk under Annex III, you become a <em>system-level</em> provider owing Chapter III duties for that system — whose application the Digital Omnibus moved to <strong>2 December 2027</strong> for Annex III standalone systems (Art. 6(2) read with Annex III).</li><li><strong>Open-source users are not exempt from copyright or summary duties.</strong> Even if your stack is built on open weights, the underlying provider still owes the Art. 53(1)(c)–(d) duties; verify the model you depend on actually meets them.</li></ul>\n<p>For the full set of deployer duties and the documentation that survives an audit, see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">EU AI Act compliance checklist for deployers</a>, and for the dates that survive the Omnibus see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-timeline-2024-2027\">EU AI Act timeline 2024–2027</a>. </p>\n",
