    "slug": "high-risk-annex-iii-classification",
    "title": "High-risk AI under Annex III: how classification works (Art. 6)",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-25",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-25",
    "description": "How the EU AI Act classifies AI systems as high-risk under Article 6: the Annex I and Annex III routes, the significant-risk carve-out (Art. 6(3)), the eight Annex III areas with examples, and the provider vs deployer split. Why classification is a 2026 skill even though Annex III high-risk obligations apply from 2 December 2027.",
    "html": "<h1>High-risk AI under Annex III: how classification works (Art. 6)</h1>\n<p>Under the EU AI Act, an AI system is <strong>high-risk</strong> when it either (a) is a safety component of — or itself is — a product covered by the Union harmonisation legislation listed in <strong>Annex I</strong> and subject to third-party conformity assessment, or (b) falls within one of the use-case areas listed in <strong>Annex III</strong> (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 6(1)–(2)</a>). The single most important point for teams in 2026 is this: <strong>classification is a skill you need now, not in 2027.</strong> The duty to <em>classify</em> your systems under Art. 6 is not delayed, and the high-risk <em>obligations</em> themselves — for providers, the conformity assessment; for deployers, the pre-use and monitoring duties — apply from <strong>2 December 2027</strong> for Annex III systems under the Digital Omnibus (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-act-and-digital-omnibus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — AI Act & Digital Omnibus</a>). You should classify today and build the evidence file now, so that when the regime switches on you are operating, not scrambling. See the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-timeline-2024-2027\">full application timeline</a>. </p>\n<h2>1. How Article 6 classifies a system as high-risk</h2>\n<p>Article 6 lays out two routes to the \"high-risk\" label, plus a carve-out and a documentation rule.</p>\n<h3>Route 1 — Annex I regulated products (Art. 6(1))</h3>\n<p>If the AI system is a <em>safety component</em> of, or <em>is itself</em>, a product governed by the Union harmonisation legislation in Annex I (for example machinery, toys, medical devices, lifts) and that product must undergo a third-party conformity assessment before being placed on the market, the AI system is high-risk (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 6(1)</a>). This route is mostly hit by AI embedded in physical or regulated products.</p>\n<h3>Route 2 — Annex III use-case areas (Art. 6(2))</h3>\n<p>This is the route most B2B SaaS hits. An AI system <em>intended for</em> one of the purposes listed in <strong>Annex III</strong> — recruitment, credit scoring, education, employment monitoring, biometrics, and more — is high-risk (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 6(2)</a>). The trigger is the <em>intended purpose</em>, not the model's capability: a general model used for CV screening inside a recruitment tool lands in Annex III by how it is deployed, not by what it theoretically can do. The annotated text of Art. 6 and Annex III is available from the <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/6/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EU AI Act Explorer</a>.</p>\n<h3>The significant-risk carve-out (Art. 6(3))</h3>\n<p>An Annex III system is <strong>not</strong> high-risk where it does not pose a <em>significant risk of harm</em> to the health, safety or fundamental rights of natural persons, including by not materially influencing the outcome of decision-making. The carve-out applies where any of four conditions is met (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 6(3)</a>):</p>\n<ul><li>(a) it performs a <strong>narrow procedural task</strong>;</li><li>(b) it <strong>improves the result of a previously completed human activity</strong>;</li><li>(c) it <strong>detects decision-making patterns or deviations</strong> without replacing or influencing the prior human assessment without proper human review; or</li><li>(d) it performs a <strong>preparatory task</strong> to an assessment relevant to an Annex III use case.</li></ul>\n<p>Two things to keep straight: first, <strong>profiling of natural persons is always high-risk</strong> notwithstanding the carve-out. Second, the carve-out is a <em>provider</em> call, not a free pass — a provider who treats an Annex III system as not-high-risk must <strong>document that assessment</strong> before placing the system on the market or in service and is subject to the registration obligation in Art. 49(2), and must hand the documentation to national competent authorities on request (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 6(4)</a>).</p>\n<h3>Who decides, and what guidance exists</h3>\n<p>Providers <strong>self-classify</strong> under Art. 6; market surveillance authorities can challenge the classification. The Commission was required to publish practical guidelines — with a list of high-risk and not-high-risk examples — by <strong>2 February 2026</strong>, after consulting the European Artificial Intelligence Board (Art. 6(5)). As of mid-2026, the Commission has issued guidance on applying Art. 6 (draft guidelines were published in May 2026); the_consistent working reference is that guidance, though the final consolidated picture depends on the amending regulation's publication. The <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-office\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">European AI Office</a> supports the <em>coherent application</em> of the AI Act across Member States, develops tools and methodologies, and prepares guidance, implementing and delegated acts — but it does not adjudicate individual classifications. Treat classification as a documented engineering and legal judgement, not a stamp you request.</p>\n<h2>2. The eight Annex III areas (with examples)</h2>\n<p>High-risk systems under Art. 6(2) are those listed in any of the following Annex III areas. For biometrics, law enforcement and migration, the use must be permitted under relevant Union or national law (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Annex III</a>).</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Annex III area</th><th>Representative examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Biometrics</strong></td><td>Remote biometric identification; biometric categorisation by sensitive or protected attributes; emotion recognition. (Biometric <em>verification</em> that only confirms a person's identity is excluded.)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Critical infrastructure</strong></td><td>Safety components in the management/operation of road traffic; water; gas; heating; electricity; and critical digital infrastructure.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Education and vocational training</strong></td><td>Admission/assignment to institutions; evaluating learning outcomes; assessing the appropriate education level; monitoring behaviour during tests.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Employment, workers management and access to self-employment</strong></td><td>Recruitment/selection, targeted job ads, CV filtering and candidate evaluation; decisions on terms, promotion or termination; task allocation by behaviour or traits; monitoring and evaluating performance.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Essential private and public services and benefits</strong></td><td>Eligibility for public assistance, benefits and healthcare; creditworthiness / credit scoring (except fraud detection); life and health insurance risk assessment and pricing; emergency-call classification and dispatch triage.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Law enforcement</strong></td><td>Assessing a person's risk of being a crime victim; polygraph-like tools; evaluating reliability of evidence; assessing offending / re-offending risk; profiling in detection and investigation.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Migration, asylum and border control</strong></td><td>Polygraph-like tools; security / irregular-migration / health risk assessment; asylum and visa examination; detecting or identifying persons (except travel-document verification).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><strong>Administration of justice and democratic processes</strong></td><td>Assisting judicial authorities in researching and interpreting facts and law; influencing the outcome of an election or referendum or the voting behaviour of natural persons (excluding administrative campaign tooling not directly exposed to persons).</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A practical note: most enterprise AI touches area 4 (recruitment, CV filtering, performance monitoring) or area 5 (credit scoring, insurance, benefits eligibility). If your system's <em>intended purpose</em> maps to any row above, start the high-risk analysis regardless of model size.</p>\n<h2>3. What deployers (and providers) should do now</h2>\n<p>The deadline for the high-risk <em>obligations</em> is <strong>2 December 2027</strong> for Annex III systems (the Digital Omnibus moved them from 2 August 2026). But the <em>classification</em> step is not delayed, and the preparation it unlocks is what makes 2027 uneventful.</p>\n<h3>For deployers — classify and build the file now</h3>\n<p>You do not have to wait. Classify every system you operate against Art. 6 and Annex III today, and keep a classification file: the system, its intended purpose, the Annex it maps to (if any), and your reasoning. Retain the provider's instructions for use and technical documentation (Art. 11 / Art. 13) as your evidence base. When the high-risk regime switches on, your pre-use and operating duties become live — the fundamental-rights and intended-purpose assessment before first use (Art. 26(2)), a qualified person and human oversight (Art. 26(3) + Art. 14), relevant and representative input data (Art. 26(4)), performance monitoring, logging and serious-incident reporting (Art. 26(5) + Art. 12 + Art. 72), and registration of your use in the EU database (Art. 26(7) + Art. 49 / Art. 71). For the operating playbook, see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">deployer compliance checklist</a>. </p>\n<h3>Provider vs deployer — who owes what</h3>\n<p>The split matters because the two sides owe different things for the same high-risk system. The <strong>provider</strong> must place a <em>conforming</em> system on the market: risk-management system (Art. 9), data governance (Art. 10), technical documentation (Art. 11), conformity assessment (Art. 43), the EU declaration of conformity and CE marking (Art. 47 / Art. 48), and registration in the EU database (Art. 49). The <strong>deployer</strong> <em>uses</em> it correctly: follows the instructions, runs the fundamental-rights check, keeps a human in the loop, monitors, and registers its own use. The deployer relies on the provider's EU declaration of conformity (Art. 47) and technical documentation (Art. 11). For the full breakdown, see <a href=\"/blog/provider-vs-deployer-responsibilities\">provider vs deployer responsibilities</a>. </p>\n<h3>For providers — start the build, not just the clock</h3>\n<p>The conformity obligations also apply from 2 December 2027 for Annex III, but the <em>build</em> work (Art. 9–15) is what makes a system conform. Starting the classification and the quality system now de-risks the 2027 deadline far more than waiting.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Planning caveat.</strong> The 2 December 2027 date assumes the Digital Omnibus amending regulation is published in the Official Journal. Until then the original 2 August 2026 application date remains the law on the books; we treat 2 Dec 2027 as the operative planning date per the Commission's stated timeline, and you should re-confirm against the published text before relying on it for binding decisions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n",
