    "description": "What the EU AI Act Art. 2(12) free and open-source exception actually covers, when it stops applying (high-risk, safety component, Art. 50 transparency, GPAI Title IV), and why open-weight models and deployers are not exempt.",
    "html": "<h1>Open-source AI and the EU AI Act: the Art. 2(12) exception, busted myths</h1>\n<p>Article 2(12) of Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 creates a carve-out: the AI Act does <strong>not</strong> apply to <em>free and open-source AI components</em> — including, where relevant, their open-weight models, software, tools and processes — that are provided under a free and open-source licence (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 2(12)</a>). The point most teams miss is that this is an exemption for the <strong>provider of the open-source component</strong>, and it switches off the moment that component is <em>placed on the EU market or put into service</em> as a high-risk AI system, a safety component of a product, an Article 50 transparency system, or a general-purpose AI (GPAI) model caught by Title IV. <strong>Open source is not a free pass</strong> — shipping an open-weight model, or building a product on one, can trigger provider and/or deployer obligations.</p>\n<h2>1. The real scope of the Art. 2(12) exception</h2>\n<p>The exception covers components that are shared openly before they are turned into a covered market or service offering. Recital 34 frames the carve-out as supporting research, innovation and the free flow of open components (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Recital 34</a>).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>What it protects.</strong> A developer who publishes model weights and code on an open licence (e.g. Apache 2.0, MIT) and does <strong>not</strong> place them on the market as a high-risk system, safety component, Article 50 system, or GPAI-under-Title-IV generally owes <strong>no</strong> AI Act obligations for that release (Art. 2(12)). This is the \"free and open-source AI components\" safe harbour.</li><li><strong>Research and pre-market work stay out.</strong> Separate exclusions also keep purely scientific R&D out of scope (Art. 2(6)) and exclude research, testing and development <em>before</em> a system is placed on the market or put into service — with real-world testing being the exception (Art. 2(8)).</li><li><strong>\"Non-commercial\" is not a permanent shield.</strong> The trigger is the <em>act</em> of placing on the market or putting into service in a covered capacity — not the licence or the team's intent. The exception is conditional by design (Art. 2(12)).</li><li><strong>Who it belongs to.</strong> The exemption attaches to the <em>provider of the open component</em>. It does not, by itself, shield a separate business that later deploys that component inside its own product (see §3).</li></ul>\n<h2>2. When the exception stops applying</h2>\n<p>Article 2(12) lists the moments the exemption ends. In each case the Act applies to whoever does the placing or putting-into-service:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>High-risk AI system (Art. 6(1)/(2), Annex I / Annex III).</strong> Once an open component is placed on the market or put into service as high-risk, the full high-risk regime attaches — provider build-side duties (Art. 8–15) and deployer duties (Art. 26). For <strong>Annex III</strong> standalone systems, those duties apply from <strong>2 December 2027</strong> under the Digital Omnibus; <strong>Annex I</strong> product-safety systems move to <strong>2 August 2028</strong> (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-act-and-digital-omnibus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — AI Act & Digital Omnibus</a>). This timing depends on the amending regulation being published in the Official Journal; until then the original dates remain on the books.</li><li><strong>Safety component of a product (Art. 6(1), Annex I).</strong> An open model used as the AI safety component of a regulated product is pulled into the same high-risk regime (Art. 2(12)).</li><li><strong>Article 50 transparency system.</strong> Open chatbots, synthetic-content and deepfake tooling are caught by the transparency duties (Art. 50), which applied from 2 August 2026 and are <strong>unchanged</strong> by the Omnibus (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — regulatory framework</a>).</li><li><strong>Title IV GPAI obligations (Chapter III).</strong> The exception ends when the open component is a GPAI model placed on the EU market — <strong>except</strong> the Article 51(2)/(3) written-exemption obligation, which does not apply (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 2(12)</a>).</li></ul>\n<h3>Open-weight foundation models are NOT exempt from the GPAI rules</h3>\n<p>This is the most common misconception. Under Title IV:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>All GPAI providers</strong> — open or closed — owe Article 53 duties: technical documentation (Art. 53(1)(a)), information to downstream providers (Art. 53(1)(b)), a copyright policy (Art. 53(1)(d)), and a public training-data summary (Art. 53(1)(c)) (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 53</a>).</li><li><strong>Open-source GPAI providers keep the minimum duties.</strong> Free/open-licence GPAI providers still must meet the copyright obligation (Art. 53(1)(d)) and publish the training-data summary (Art. 53(1)(c)); they are not relieved of the other Article 53 obligations (<a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/high-level-summary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AI Act Explorer — high-level summary</a>).</li><li><strong>Systemic-risk GPAI models owe everything.</strong> A GPAI model presumed systemic-risk at ≥10²⁵ floating-point operations (Art. 51 / Art. 52) — open or closed — owes the full Article 55 set: model evaluation, adversarial testing, serious-incident reporting and cybersecurity (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 55</a>). An open-weight frontier model is fully in scope.</li><li><strong>Timing.</strong> GPAI provider obligations applied from 2 August 2025 (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — regulatory framework</a>).</li></ul>\n<h2>3. What this means for open-source providers and deployers</h2>\n<p><strong>Deployers are not shielded by open source.</strong> A company that takes an open model and puts it into service inside its own product is a <strong>deployer</strong> under Article 26 — whether or not it built the model (Art. 26(1); Art. 3(6)) (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 26</a>). The Art. 2(12) exemption belongs to the component's provider, not to the business deploying it. For the baseline deployer duties, see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">EU AI Act compliance checklist for deployers</a>. </p>\n<p>Deployer baseline duties that apply <strong>now</strong> (not postponed): AI literacy for staff who operate AI (Art. 4), use each system per the provider's instructions (Art. 26(1)), transparency to affected people (Art. 26(6) + Art. 50), and retaining the provider's technical documentation (Art. 11 / Art. 13). The high-risk deployer duties (Art. 26(2)–(7)) phase in with the high-risk regime — <strong>2 December 2027</strong> for Annex III (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-act-and-digital-omnibus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — AI Act & Digital Omnibus</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>The provider/deployer split still applies to OSS.</strong> If you fine-tune an open model and place the resulting system on the EU market, you may become a <strong>provider</strong> (Art. 25) owing the build-side duties — risk management (Art. 9), data governance (Art. 10), technical documentation (Art. 11). The open-source origin of the base model does not relieve you (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 25</a>). For the full split, see <a href=\"/blog/provider-vs-deployer-responsibilities\">provider vs deployer responsibilities</a>. </p>\n<p><strong>Myths to retire:</strong></p>\n<ul><li><em>\"Open source means no obligations.\"</em> False — the exemption is conditional and ends on market placement or in-service use in a covered role (Art. 2(12)).</li><li><em>\"I'm just using an open model, so I'm safe.\"</em> False for deployers — Article 26 applies regardless of who built the model (Art. 26(1)).</li><li><em>\"Open-weight LLMs are exempt.\"</em> False — Title IV applies; open providers owe at least Art. 53(1)(c)/(d), and systemic-risk models owe all of Art. 55 (Art. 53 / Art. 55).</li><li><em>\"Research / non-commercial use keeps me safe forever.\"</em> Mostly true pre-market (Art. 2(6), Art. 2(8)), but placing the component on the EU market or into service as high-risk / safety / Art. 50 / GPAI ends the exemption (Art. 2(12)).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Watch-items and uncertainty (as of July 2026).</strong> The Digital Omnibus (Council approval 29 June 2026) delays the <em>application</em> of the high-risk regime — Annex III to 2 Dec 2027, Annex I to 2 Aug 2028 (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-act-and-digital-omnibus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — AI Act & Digital Omnibus</a>). Article 2(12)'s exception language is <strong>not</strong> among the provisions reported as amended by the Omnibus; the trigger logic is unchanged — but confirm against the consolidated amending regulation once published in the Official Journal. Draft Commission guidelines on high-risk classification (19 May 2026) inform the Art. 6 determination. Penalty exposure (Art. 99) is a public reference point only. For the defensible calendar of application dates, see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-timeline-2024-2027\">EU AI Act timeline 2024–2027</a>. </p>\n",
