    "description": "How the EU AI Act splits responsibility between providers (Art. 3(3), Art. 25) and deployers (Art. 3(4), Art. 26), with the edge cases of fine-tuning, building your own system, and the authorised representative under Art. 22.",
    "html": "<h1>Provider vs deployer responsibilities under the EU AI Act</h1>\n<p>A <strong>provider</strong> under the EU AI Act is the party that develops an AI system or has one developed and places it on the market or puts it into service under its own name or trademark (Art. 3(3); Recital 78). A <strong>deployer</strong> is any natural or legal person, public authority, agency or other body that uses an AI system under its authority, except where the system is used in the course of a personal, non-professional activity (Art. 3(4); Recital 13). The decisive test is not who wrote the code but <em>who places the system on the market</em> versus <em>who operates it inside their own business</em>: the provider owns the build and the conformity of the system (Art. 25; Arts. 9–16), while the deployer owns correct use, human oversight and monitoring (Art. 26, Art. 27). The same organisation can sit in both seats at once, and the split itself is not delayed by the Digital Omnibus — only the application of the high-risk regime for Annex III standalone systems moved to 2 December 2027. For the deployer-side actions, see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">EU AI Act compliance checklist for deployers</a>. </p>\n<h2>1. Definitions and boundaries (Art. 3(3) vs Art. 3(4))</h2>\n<p>The AI Act draws the line between the two roles on the basis of function, not on who authored the model. Both definitions sit in Article 3 and are read together with the operating duties in Articles 25 and 26 (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EUR-Lex, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 3</a>; <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AI Act Explorer, Art. 3</a>).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Provider (Art. 3(3)).</strong> Develops an AI system or has it developed, and places it on the market or puts it into service under its own name or trademark — whether for payment or free of charge. This covers both the builder and the entity that commissions a build and markets it.</li><li><strong>Deployer (Art. 3(4)).</strong> Uses an AI system under its authority for a professional purpose. Consuming a third-party model via API, or running an off-the-shelf tool, makes you a deployer even though you built nothing.</li><li><strong>Out of scope.</strong> Individuals using AI outside work (personal, non-professional activity) are not deployers under Art. 3(4).</li></ul>\n<h3>Provider vs deployer at a glance</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Provider (Art. 3(3); Art. 25)</th><th>Deployer (Art. 3(4); Art. 26)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Core role</td><td>Develops or has developed, and places on the market / puts into service under own name or trademark</td><td>Uses the system under its authority for a professional purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Owns the build</td><td>Yes — risk management (Art. 9), data governance (Art. 10), technical documentation (Art. 11)</td><td>No — consumes the provider's output</td></tr><tr><td>Conformity</td><td>Conformity assessment (Art. 43), EU declaration of conformity (Art. 47/48)</td><td>Relies on the provider's conformity</td></tr><tr><td>Transparency duty</td><td>Give instructions for use to deployers (Art. 13)</td><td>Follow those instructions (Art. 26(1))</td></tr><tr><td>High-risk pre-use step</td><td>Build the system to the requirements (Art. 9–15)</td><td>Fundamental-rights impact assessment where required (Art. 27(1)) + intended-use check</td></tr><tr><td>Post-market</td><td>Post-market monitoring (Art. 72, provider side)</td><td>Monitor performance, keep logs, report serious incidents (Art. 26(5), Art. 12, Art. 72)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The European Commission frames the same divide as \"providers\" who put systems on the market and \"deployers\" who ensure human oversight and monitoring once a system is in use (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — AI Act regulatory framework</a>).</p>\n<h2>2. Core obligations: what each role owes (Art. 25 vs Art. 26)</h2>\n<h3>2.1 Provider obligations (Art. 25 and Arts. 9–16)</h3>\n<p>The provider owes the <em>build</em>: the system must be constructed to the horizontal requirements before it reaches the market.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Risk management system (Art. 9):</strong> a continuous process across the lifecycle, from design to post-market.</li><li><strong>Data and data governance (Art. 10):</strong> training, validation and testing data must meet quality and relevance standards.</li><li><strong>Technical documentation (Art. 11):</strong> the evidence package authorities use to assess conformity.</li><li><strong>Record-keeping / logs (Art. 12):</strong> automatic logging for traceability of high-risk systems.</li><li><strong>Transparency and instructions for use (Art. 13):</strong> the provider must supply clear instructions so the deployer can use the system correctly — this is the hand-off that defines the deployer's duty in Art. 26(1).</li><li><strong>Human oversight by design (Art. 14)</strong> and <strong>accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity (Art. 15).</strong></li><li><strong>Provider obligations for high-risk systems (Art. 16):</strong> a quality management system, the conformity assessment (Art. 43), the EU declaration of conformity, and registration.</li><li><strong>Value-chain responsibilities (Art. 25):</strong> where a provider subcontracts or a downstream party makes a <em>substantial modification</em>, responsibility follows the change. The initial provider must cooperate and hand over the information needed for conformity (<a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AI Act Explorer, Art. 25</a>).</li></ul>\n<p>Whether a system is high-risk in the first place — and therefore which build duties apply — is decided by the classification logic. See <a href=\"/blog/high-risk-annex-iii-classification\">Annex III categories and the Art. 6 classification logic</a>. </p>\n<h3>2.2 Deployer obligations (Art. 26, plus Art. 27 for certain deployers)</h3>\n<p>The deployer owes the <em>use</em>: operating the system as the provider intended, with oversight and monitoring.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Use per the provider's instructions for use (Art. 26(1)).</strong> The central deployer duty: operate within the documented intended purpose and constraints.</li><li><strong>Human oversight (Art. 26(3) read with Art. 14):</strong> assign natural persons to oversee the system and keep them in the loop.</li><li><strong>Input data relevance (Art. 26(4)):</strong> ensure the data the system processes relates to the intended purpose and is representative.</li><li><strong>Monitoring, logs and incident reporting (Art. 26(5), Art. 12, Art. 72):</strong> track performance, keep records, and report serious incidents to the provider and, where required, to the market surveillance authority.</li><li><strong>Transparency to affected people (Art. 26(6) read with Art. 50):</strong> tell natural persons when they interact with an AI system and when content is artificially generated or manipulated.</li><li><strong>EU database registration (Art. 26(7) read with Art. 49 / Art. 71):</strong> high-risk deployers register their use in the public EU database.</li><li><strong>Fundamental rights impact assessment (Art. 27(1)):</strong> required of bodies governed by public law, private entities providing public services, and deployers in Annex III points 5(b) and 5(c), prior to deployment.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Timing note.</strong> For standalone high-risk systems listed in Annex III, these high-risk deployer duties — the fundamental-rights assessment (Art. 27(1)), human oversight (Art. 26(3)), incident reporting (Art. 26(5)) and EU database registration (Art. 26(7)) — now apply from <strong>2 December 2027</strong>, not 2 August 2026, under the Digital Omnibus (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/eu-agrees-simplify-ai-rules-boost-innovation-and-ban-nudification-apps-protect-citizens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — simplification agreement</a>). Systems embedded in regulated products (Annex I) move to 2 August 2028. This depends on the amending regulation's publication in the Official Journal; until then the original 2 August 2026 date remains the law on the books. The role split and the always-on duties (Art. 4 literacy, Art. 5 prohibitions, Art. 50 transparency) are unaffected. For the full calendar, see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-timeline-2024-2027\">EU AI Act timeline 2024–2027</a>. </p>\n<h2>3. Boundary edge cases</h2>\n<h3>3.1 Fine-tuning someone else's model</h3>\n<p>The question \"am I a provider or a deployer?\" turns on whether your change is <em>substantial</em>. If you take a model already on the market and make a substantial modification such that it remains a high-risk system under Art. 6, Art. 25(1)(b) treats you as a provider for that modified system and you owe the full build duties (Art. 9–16, Art. 43). A lighter adaptation that does not change the risk classification keeps you in the deployer seat. This depends on whether the change qualifies as \"substantial\" — as of July 2026 the Commission had not published final modification guidance, so borderline cases should be assessed per Art. 25(1) with qualified EU counsel.</p>\n<h3>3.2 Building and deploying your own system</h3>\n<p>If you both develop an AI system and operate AI systems built by others, you wear two hats at once: provider duties for what you place on the market (Art. 25) and deployer duties for what you use (Art. 26). The two roles are assessed separately; the obligations do not merge or cancel out.</p>\n<h3>3.3 Non-EU providers and the authorised representative (Art. 22)</h3>\n<p>Providers established outside the Union must appoint an authorised representative established in the EU before placing a high-risk AI system on the market (Art. 22). The representative keeps the technical documentation for 10 years, supplies it to authorities on request, cooperates on risk management, and must inform the authorities if it terminates the mandate. The deployer's duties are unchanged — a deployer owes Art. 26 regardless of where the provider is based (<a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/22/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AI Act Explorer, Art. 22</a>).</p>\n",
