    "slug": "what-digital-omnibus-changed",
    "title": "What the Digital Omnibus actually changed in the EU AI Act",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-25",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-25",
    "description": "What the EU AI Act Digital Omnibus actually changed: Annex III high-risk obligations move to 2 December 2027 and Annex I to 2 August 2028, while AI literacy, prohibitions, transparency and GPAI rules stay on their original schedules. A cited, decision-support explainer.",
    "html": "<h1>What the Digital Omnibus actually changed in the EU AI Act</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Digital Omnibus on AI</strong> is the EU's amending regulation (Legislative Procedure 2025/0359(COD), Commission proposal COM(2025) 836) that adjusts <em>how and when</em> parts of the AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) apply — not whether the Act exists. Its headline effect: the high-risk regime for <strong>standalone systems listed in Annex III</strong> now applies from <strong>2 December 2027</strong> instead of 2 August 2026, and the high-risk rules for <strong>AI embedded in regulated products (Annex I)</strong> move to <strong>2 August 2028</strong>. What it does <em>not</em> do is repeal the Act or waive the always-on duties — AI literacy (Art. 4), prohibited practices (Art. 5), transparency (Art. 50), and the general-purpose AI rules (Art. 53 / Art. 55) remain in force on their original schedules. If you only remember one sentence: the Omnibus delays <em>some application dates</em>, it does not delete <em>obligations</em>. For the full calendar, see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-timeline-2024-2027\">EU AI Act application timeline</a>. </p>\n<h2>1. What the Digital Omnibus delayed</h2>\n<p>The delay is narrow and structural. It moves the <em>application</em> of the high-risk regime forward; it does not touch the text of the obligations themselves. The dates below assume the amending regulation is published in the Official Journal — the Commission targeted publication before 2 August 2026, and the Council gave final approval in late June 2026 after the European Parliament adopted the text on 16 June 2026 (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/eu-agrees-simplify-ai-rules-boost-innovation-and-ban-nudification-apps-protect-citizens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — EU agrees to simplify AI rules</a>; <a href=\"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC AI Act Service Desk — implementation timeline</a>). Until that publication, the original 2 August 2026 deadline remains the law on the books.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Regime</th><th>Original application date</th><th>New application date (Digital Omnibus)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Annex III high-risk</strong> — standalone systems (recruitment, education, employment, essential services, biometrics, migration/asylum, border control, law enforcement, justice, critical infrastructure)</td><td>2 Aug 2026</td><td><strong>2 Dec 2027</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Annex I high-risk</strong> — AI embedded in, or acting as a safety component of, regulated products (medical devices, machinery, toys, lifts, vehicles)</td><td>2 Aug 2027</td><td><strong>2 Aug 2028</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<ul><li><strong>What moved:</strong> the provider-side duties that make up the high-risk regime — risk management system (Art. 9), data governance (Art. 10), technical documentation (Art. 11), logging and record-keeping (Art. 12), accuracy and robustness (Art. 15), human oversight (Art. 14), conformity assessment (Art. 43), and EU database registration (Art. 49 / Art. 71) — now bite on the new dates for these systems (<a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32024R1689\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Arts. 9, 10, 11, 43</a>).</li><li><strong>Fixed dates, not a stop-the-clock.</strong> The agreed text uses fixed calendar dates rather than a conditional trigger tied to the availability of harmonised standards. There is no moving target that springs forward the moment a standard is published (<a href=\"https://www.europarl.europa.eu/legislative-train/package-digital-package/file-digital-omnibus-on-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">European Parliament — Legislative Train on the Digital Omnibus on AI</a>).</li><li><strong>The classification analysis is not delayed.</strong> Determining <em>whether</em> your system is high-risk (Art. 6, read with Annex III or Annex I) is work you owe now; only the <em>consequences</em> of a high-risk classification are postponed. See <a href=\"/blog/high-risk-annex-iii-classification\">high-risk classification under Annex III</a>. </li></ul>\n<h2>2. What the Digital Omnibus did NOT change</h2>\n<p>The amending regulation leaves the following in place. For B2B teams, these are the obligations you feel now or soon regardless of the high-risk delay:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Art. 4 — AI literacy.</strong> In force since 2 February 2025; providers and deployers must ensure staff operating or using AI have a sufficient level of AI literacy. Untouched (<a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EC — regulatory framework for AI</a>).</li><li><strong>Art. 5 — prohibited practices.</strong> In force since 2 February 2025; the unacceptable-risk bans are unchanged. The Omnibus <em>adds</em> a new prohibition on AI systems that generate non-consensual intimate or sexual content and child sexual abuse material, applying from 2 December 2026.</li><li><strong>Art. 50 — transparency.</strong> Applies from 2 August 2026: deployers must disclose when people interact with an AI system, and providers must mark AI-generated content. The only concession is a grace period — systems already on the market before 2 August 2026 have until 2 December 2026 to meet the machine-readable marking requirement (Art. 50(2)).</li><li><strong>GPAI rules — Art. 53 / Art. 55 (Chapter V).</strong> The obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models remain in force from 2 August 2025 and are <strong>not</strong> delayed by the Omnibus. Note the distinction: Annex I covers AI <em>embedded in regulated products</em> (moving to 2 August 2028); GPAI is a separate Chapter V category and was not postponed.</li><li><strong>Art. 9 / 10 / 11 / 13 / 14 / 15 — the substantive high-risk requirements.</strong> These articles were not rewritten. They already bind GPAI providers today, and they will bind high-risk providers from the delayed dates above. They are <em>delayed in application</em>, not deleted.</li></ul>\n<blockquote><p><strong>\"Unchanged\" means two things.</strong> First, the article text and the already-in-force instances (Art. 4, Art. 5, Art. 50, Chapter V GPAI) are untouched. Second, for an Annex III or Annex I provider, Art. 9 / 10 / 11 / 13 simply do not <em>bite</em> until 2 December 2027 or 2 August 2028 — but the day that date arrives, the same articles apply unchanged. The Omnibus buys time, not exemption.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>3. What it means for providers and deployers</h2>\n<p>For <strong>deployers (Art. 26)</strong>, the immediately relevant duties are the always-on ones — AI literacy (Art. 4), using each system in line with the provider's instructions (Art. 26(1)), transparency to affected people (Art. 26(6) + Art. 50), and retaining the provider's technical documentation and instructions (Art. 11 / Art. 13). The heavy high-risk deployer duties — fundamental-rights and intended-purpose assessment (Art. 26(2)), qualified-person and human-oversight operation (Art. 26(3) + Art. 14), input-data relevance (Art. 26(4)), monitoring, logging and serious-incident reporting (Art. 26(5) + Art. 12 + Art. 72), and EU high-risk database registration (Art. 26(7) + Art. 49 / Art. 71) — phase in with the high-risk regime: <strong>2 December 2027</strong> for Annex III systems. For the full split, see the <a href=\"/blog/eu-ai-act-compliance-checklist-deployers\">EU AI Act compliance checklist for deployers</a>. </p>\n<p>For <strong>providers (Art. 25)</strong> placing a high-risk Annex III system on the EU market, the build-side duties (Art. 9 / 10 / 11 / 12 / 14 / 15, conformity assessment under Art. 43) now have until 2 December 2027 to be fully in application for that category. But the underlying work — risk management, data governance, technical documentation — is the same work you would do for a GPAI model today, so starting now is cheaper than a 2027 fire drill.</p>\n<p>Practical guidance:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Classify now (Art. 6).</strong> Establish whether your system is high-risk (Annex III under Art. 6(2), or Annex I under Art. 6(1)). The classification analysis is not delayed.</li><li><strong>Stand up the foundational layer now.</strong> AI literacy (Art. 4), transparency (Art. 50), prohibitions (Art. 5), and GPAI duties if you publish a general-purpose model (Art. 53 / 55) all bite on their original dates.</li><li><strong>Use the extra time deliberately.</strong> For high-risk systems, build the evidence store (Art. 9 / 10 / 11 / 12) so you are ready for 2 Dec 2027 / 2 Aug 2028 rather than scrambling at the deadline.</li><li><strong>Do not read \"delay\" as \"exempt.\"</strong> The Act is in force; enforcement of prohibitions, transparency, and GPAI is active from 2026, and penalty powers apply (Art. 99).</li></ol>\n",
